l thousand.\n\nBut regardless, you have a strong hand.\n\nBest actions:\n- Check (0): let showdown. Low risk, but lose value.\n- Bet 100: small value bet — likely to get called by weaker hands.\n- Bet larger (e.g., 1000+): might induce fold from weaker hands, but may not get more calls from worse.\n\nSince opponent raised on turn, they likely have *some* hand — maybe top pair or draw. Now checked on river — could be giving up.\n\nA small bet (100) can get called by:\n- J-x (top pair)\n- 9-x\n- Bluff catchers\n\nAnd you beat all of them.\n\nGoing all-in (-2) is ~18k — too big, will only get called by better hands (if they have 10-4 or flush), so bad.\n\nFolding (-1) is insane — you have a straight.\n\nChecking (0) lets worse hands realize equity but gives free showdown — why give free showdown?\n\nSo **betting 100** is optimal — minimum bet, maximum value extraction from marginal hands.\n\nReason:  \nYou have a five-high straight, a strong hand that likely beats any pair or missed draw. Opponent showed strength on the turn but checked on the river, indicating possible weakness. A small bet of 100 maximizes the chance of being called by worse hands while extracting value. Larger bets risk folding out worse hands without gaining much extra from stronger ones.\n\nAnswer: 100"}
